Placental Abruption
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Severe continuous abdominal pain, Uterine rigidity/hypertonus, Fetal distress

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-033
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, continuous abdominal pain and a rigid, board-like abdomen at 35 weeks gestation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated sudden onset of excruciating, unremitting abdominal pain, dark vaginal bleeding, and a rock-hard abdomen in a third-trimester pregnancy. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to instantly recognize a catastrophic placental abruption, initiate immediate resuscitation protocols, and expedite priority 1 emergency transport to prevent imminent fetal demise and maternal hemorrhagic shock.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My stomach suddenly clamped down completely hard, the pain is unbearable and will not stop, and I just passed some dark blood.
History of Present Illness: The patient, currently at 35 weeks gestation, reports the sudden onset of severe, continuous abdominal and pelvic pain that began 45 minutes ago. She describes the pain as a constant, tearing sensation without any relief between contractions. She reports a small amount of dark red vaginal bleeding, though she feels faint, suggesting the bleeding may be largely concealed. She states she has not felt the baby move since the pain started. She reports a history of pregnancy-induced hypertension.
Functional Status: Lying on the floor, curled in a fetal position, writhing in severe pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate, life-threatening obstetric emergency; risk of fetal demise and maternal Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC).

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/50 mmHg | HR: 125 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Patient is profoundly pale, diaphoretic, tachypneic, and in extreme agony.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals severe uterine hypertonus; the abdomen is board-like, rigid, and exquisitely tender to light palpation across the entire fundus. There is no relaxation of the uterine muscle. Fetal parts cannot be palpated due to the extreme rigidity. Fetal heart rate auscultation via handheld Doppler reveals severe bradycardia (approximately 80 bpm), indicating profound fetal distress. Visual inspection of undergarments confirms a small amount of dark, clotted blood. The patient exhibits maternal tachycardia and hypotension disproportionate to the visible blood loss, highly indicative of massive concealed hemorrhage.
Environmental Observations: Clear path for stretcher; front door unlocked for paramedics.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking labetalol for hypertension.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Placental Abruption with concealed hemorrhage and fetal distress - Status: Critical/Crashing
Differentials:

Placenta Praevia - Definitively ruled out by the presence of severe, agonizing pain, a rigid, board-like uterus, and dark (rather than bright red) bleeding.

Uterine Rupture - A critical differential with similar presentation (severe pain, fetal distress), but usually involves a loss of fetal station or palpable fetal parts just under the skin; regardless, management (immediate surgery) is identical.

Severe Pre-eclampsia / HELLP Syndrome - While the patient has hypertension, the acute, sudden rigid abdomen and bradycardia point to an immediate mechanical/vascular catastrophe (abruption), though HELLP may be a co-morbidity.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Imminent threat to dual life)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is presenting with a catastrophic, life-threatening obstetric emergency. The diagnostic tetrad of severe continuous abdominal pain, a board-like rigid uterus, dark vaginal bleeding, and profound fetal bradycardia (80 bpm) is pathognomonic for a severe placental abruption with massive concealed hemorrhage. The patient is entering hypovolemic shock. Urgent after-hours presence was required to instantly recognize this time-critical emergency, immediately activate priority EMS transport (000), position the patient to maximize remaining placental perfusion, and prepare for potential maternal resuscitation. This situation mandates immediate "crash" cesarean section at a tertiary facility; any delay equates to fetal, and potentially maternal, mortality.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately activated Emergency Medical Services (000) for a "Priority 1 - Obstetric Emergency / Suspected Abruption" transport. Placed the patient in a strict left lateral decubitus position and administered high-flow oxygen (15L via non-rebreather mask found in GP emergency bag) to maximize fetal oxygenation. Secured a large-bore (14G) peripheral IV cannula while awaiting paramedics to facilitate immediate fluid/blood resuscitation upon their arrival.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maximize the remaining oxygen reaching your baby, you must remain strictly on your left side and breathe deeply through the oxygen mask; understanding that this is the only way to help the baby right now will ensure compliance despite the severe pain. To prepare for immediate surgery upon arrival at the hospital, remain strictly nil by mouth; understanding this prevents fatal aspiration under general anesthetic will ensure strict fasting.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Emergency Medical Services (Ambulance) activated and on scene.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Direct, urgent clinical handover provided to the responding paramedic crew.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is currently under direct medical supervision awaiting immediate ambulance transport for a life-saving emergency cesarean section.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is an absolute, critical emergency requiring immediate surgery to save both your life and the baby's life.
Clinical Course: The ambulance will take you directly to the operating theater at the hospital. You will be put to sleep immediately for an emergency cesarean section.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not move from your left side. Keep the oxygen mask on tightly. Do not eat or drink anything.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your placenta has prematurely torn away from the wall of your womb. This is causing massive internal bleeding (which is why your stomach is rock hard and you feel faint) and it is cutting off the oxygen supply to your baby. We have called a priority ambulance. We are giving you pure oxygen and keeping you on your side to push every last drop of oxygen to the baby. You will require an immediate operation as soon as you reach the hospital.
Actionable Steps: Keep the oxygen mask firmly on your face. Try to take slow, deep breaths. Let the paramedics move you; do not try to help them.

